THE MUNICIPAL ARCHIVE ORDINANCE

Section 1. Purpose. The purpose of this ordinance is to establish the duties of the city archivist with respect to the retention, cataloguing, and public disclosure of municipal records, and to set out the procedure by which residents may request access to materials that are not subject to a statutory exemption.

Section 2. Definitions. For the purposes of this ordinance, the term record means any document, photograph, map, ledger, audio recording, or electronic file produced or received in the course of official business, regardless of physical form or storage medium. The term archive means the climate-controlled facility on the second floor of the civic center together with the digital repository maintained under contract with the regional library consortium. The term archivist means the officer appointed under Section 4 and any deputy designated in writing by that officer.

Section 3. Retention schedule. Every department shall transfer to the archive, no later than ninety days after the close of each fiscal year, all records that have reached the end of their scheduled retention period as set out in the schedule adopted by resolution of the council. Records subject to litigation, audit, or a pending public records request shall not be destroyed until the matter is finally resolved and the archivist has certified in writing that no further hold applies.

Section 4. Appointment and duties. The archivist shall be appointed by the mayor with the advice and consent of the council for a term of four years and may be reappointed. The archivist shall maintain an inventory of all holdings, publish an annual report describing acquisitions, losses, and conservation work, supervise the reading room during published hours, and advise departments on the proper storage of photographic and magnetic media.

Section 5. Public access. Any resident may inspect non-exempt records during regular business hours without charge. Copies shall be furnished at the fee schedule adopted annually by the council, which shall not exceed the actual cost of reproduction and mailing. Where a record contains both exempt and non-exempt material, the archivist shall redact the exempt portions and release the remainder, noting on the face of the copy the legal basis for each redaction.

Section 6. Appeals. An applicant whose request is denied in whole or in part may appeal in writing to the city manager within twenty business days of the denial. The city manager shall issue a written decision within thirty days, and that decision is subject to judicial review under the applicable state statute.

Section 7. Preservation committee. A preservation committee of five members shall meet quarterly to review the condition of fragile holdings and to recommend conservation expenditures. Members shall serve without compensation but may be reimbursed for travel at the standard municipal rate.

Section 8. Digital migration. The archivist shall ensure that records created in obsolete formats are migrated to current open standards at least once every seven years, and shall retain a checksum manifest so that the integrity of each migrated file can be verified.

Section 9. Conflict. In the event of a conflict between this ordinance and a state or federal records law, the stricter requirement shall govern.

Section 10. Effective date. This ordinance takes effect on the first day of the fiscal year following its adoption.

